  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nBroad 1025 mb surface high pressure has built south from Canada and \nis currently centered across the central Dakotas with dew points in \nthe 40s and 50s to the northwest.  Drier air will filter into the \nregion this afternoon with high temperatures in the mid 80s.  \n\nSurface ridge is expected to shift east tonight leading to good \nradiative conditions.  Have forecast lows in the upper 50s to lower \n60s across the region. With the good radiative conditions, could see \npatchy fog develop in the river valleys and drainage areas, though \nincreasing high clouds from showers and thunderstorms across the \nNorthern Plains may limit potential.  \n\nIsotropic lift will continue to support the shorts pushing east, \nthough activity will struggle the farther east it goes with limited \nmoisture.  Expect the net result to be increasing clouds, and \npotentially a few sprinkles across eastern KS.  Have lowered \nforecast highs across eastern KS into western MO with increasing \nclouds.  \n\nRidge riding short wave is expected to build south along the \nMissouri River basin Sunday night. Increasing isentropic lift near \nthe 310K surface may lead to spotty showers and thunderstorms \novernight.  Mid- level lapse rates are near moist adiabatic so \nshowers should have limited instability to work with, and dry air \nnear the surface should limit precip potential. \n\nWarmer, more muggy conditions are expected the first half of next \nweek as the long standing upper ridge expands east.  A series of \nridge riding short waves building into the Upper Midwest could lead \nto additional showers and thunderstorms mid-week.",
